rld; second, ENSwR provides a novel explanatory framework that supports origins of life theories in which autocatalytic sets of chemical reactions play a central role; third, we argue that inorganic catalytic molecules (rather than autocatalytic sets (7–9)) are relevant units of selection to understand the origins of life prior to reproduction and replication. This third argument is especially important for the field, as it helps bridge the gap between chemical and biological evolution (10)."}]", "excerpt_truncated": true, "source_sha256": "7236204601987fb279e8ff00b7c74e1b7caf967226e62f538be49442cf27cb0a", "verification_required": true, "topic_domain": "chemical_heredity", "evidence_role": "discovery", "host_tier": "discovery", "source_identity": null, "persistent_identifiers": ["doi:10.1021/jacs.3c07041", "doi:10.1101/2025.07.29.667458", "openalex:W4386823721"], "source_candidates": ["https://doi.org/10.1021/jacs.3c07041", "https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.29.667458", "https://www.biorxiv.org/content/biorxiv/early/2025/08/01/2025.07.29.667458.full.pdf"], "source_candidate_identities": {}}

